## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design distributed workflow using sagas, CQRS, and event-sourcing in real-world microservices; probes handling of failure modes, idempotency, compensations, and consistency guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration vs choreography
- Event store and projections for CQRS
- Compensating actions and timeouts
- Idempotent handlers and outbox pattern

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: saga orchestrator pattern
class CheckoutSaga {
  async start(order) {
    await dispatch('ReserveInventory', order);
  }
  async onEvent(event) {
    if (event.type === 'InventoryReserved') await dispatch('CapturePayment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'PaymentCaptured') await dispatch('ScheduleShipment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'ShipmentScheduled') complete(event.order);
    else if (event.type.endsWith('Failed')) compensate(event.order);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What are the trade-offs between orchestration and choreography in this scenario?
- How would you test the saga under partial failures and timeouts?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your understanding of API design patterns, code reuse principles, and performance optimization across different API paradigms—critical skills for building scalable microservice architectures.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates should discuss: a shared business logic layer, separate API adapters for REST and GraphQL, GraphQL data loaders for query batching, REST response caching mechanisms, and database query optimization tailored to each API type's access patterns.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Shared service layer
class UserService {
  async getUser(id: string) {
    return db.user.findUnique({ where: { id } });
  }
}

// GraphQL resolver with data loader
const userResolver = {
  user: async (_, { id }, { loaders }) => {
    return loaders.user.load(id);
  }
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design cross-protocol schema alignment and implement practical versioning strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-protocol schema mapping from canonical model
- Unified internal service layer with protocol adapters
- Consistent versioning across all protocols
- Contract validation and routing patterns

## Code Example

```javascript
// Canonical model adapter
function toInternalBook(restInput) {
  return {
    id: restInput.id || generateId(),
    title: restInput.title,
    author: restInput.author,
    stock: restInput.stock || 0
  };
}

// Protocol-agnostic service layer
const bookService = {
  async getBook(id) {
    return await database.books.findById(id);
  },
  async createBook(bookData) {
    const internalBook = toInternalBook(bookData);
    return await database.books.create(internalBook);
  }
};

// Protocol adapters
const restAdapter = {
  getBook: async (req, res) => {
    const book = await bookService.getBook(req.params.id);
    res.json(book);
  },
  createBook: async (req, res) => {
    const book = await bookService.createBook(req.body);
    res.status(201).json(book);
  }
};

// GraphQL schema
type Book {
  id: ID!
  title: String!
  author: String!
  stock: Int!
}

type Query {
  getBook(id: ID!): Book
}

type Mutation {
  addBook(title: String!, author: String!, stock: Int): Book
}

// gRPC service definition
service BookService {
  rpc GetBook(GetBookRequest) returns (Book);
  rpc CreateBook(CreateBookRequest) returns (Book);
}

// Versioning patterns
// REST: /api/v1/books
// GraphQL: Use @deprecated directive for v2 changes
// gRPC: package v1.bookservice;
```

## Implementation Strategy
1. Define canonical domain model first
2. Create protocol-specific schemas that map to the canonical model
3. Implement adapter layer for request/response transformation
4. Build unified service layer with business logic
5. Apply consistent versioning strategy across all protocols

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of sagas, event-sourcing, and idempotent orchestration across multiple services with compensating actions. It emphasizes minimal viable patterns suitable for beginner level while exposing real-world trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- In-process Saga orchestrator backed by an Event Store
- Outbox pattern for durable publication of commands
- Event contracts: OrderPlaced, PaymentApproved/Declined, InventoryReserved/Failed, OrderCompleted/Cancelled
- Idempotent handlers using correlationId; replayable history for recovery
- Compensating actions for failures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal orchestrator sketch (JS) illustrating event-to-command mapping
// Event types: OrderPlaced, PaymentApproved, InventoryReserved, etc.
class SagaState { constructor(orderId) { this.orderId = orderId; this.step = 'start'; this.status = 'in_progress'; this.completed = false; } }
const store = new Map(); // correlationId -> SagaState
function publish(cmd) { /* Outbox publish placeholder */ }
function handleEvent(e) {
  const state = store.get(e.correlationId) ?? new SagaState(e.orderId);
  if (state.completed) return;
  switch (e.type) {
    case 'OrderPlaced': state.step = 'payment'; publish({ type: 'CheckPayment', orderId: e.orderId, correlationId: e.correlationId }); break;
    case 'PaymentApproved': state.step = 'inventory'; publish({ type: 'ReserveInventory', orderId: e.orderId, correlationId: e.correlationId }); break;
    case 'InventoryReserved': state.completed = true; state.status = 'completed'; publish({ type: 'CompleteOrder', orderId: e.orderId, correlationId: e.correlationId }); break;
    case 'PaymentDeclined': case 'InventoryFailed': state.completed = true; state.status = 'cancelled'; publish({ type: 'CancelOrder', orderId: e.orderId, correlationId: e.correlationId }); break;
  }
  store.set(e.correlationId, state);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to architect a sophisticated multi-protocol API gateway that addresses enterprise-grade requirements including tenant isolation, dynamic feature management, and operational excellence at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-protocol API gateway design supporting REST, GraphQL, and gRPC
- Redis-based distributed feature flags with tenant-specific configurations
- Centralized OpenAPI specification management with versioning and deprecation strategies
- GraphQL schema stitching integrating REST and gRPC services with lazy loading and streaming
- Multi-layer data isolation through API-level and database-level policy enforcement
- Per-tenant observability using OpenTelemetry and advanced deployment patterns (canary, blue-green)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Tenant-aware GraphQL field resolver with REST backend integration
const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    userData: async (parent, { id }, context) => {
      const { tenantId, featureFlags } = context;

      // Check feature flag before proceeding
      if (!await featureFlags.isEnabled('userData', tenantId)) {
        throw new Error('Feature not available for tenant');
      }

      // Fetch from REST backend with tenant isolation
      return fetch(`/api/v1/tenants/${tenantId}/users/${id}`);
    }
  }
};

// Per-tenant OpenAPI specification fetch
class TenantSpecGenerator {
  async generateTenantSpec(tenantId, version = 'latest') {
    const baseSpec = await this.getBaseSpec();
    const tenantConfig = await this.getTenantConfig(tenantId);

    return {
      ...baseSpec,
      servers: [{ url: `https://${tenantId}.api.example.com` }],
      'x-tenant-id': tenantId,
      'x-version': version,
      paths: this.filterPathsForTenant(baseSpec.paths, tenantConfig)
    };
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests per-tenant Saga resilience, isolation, and recovery with Event Sourcing and outbox.

## Key Concepts

- CQRS and Event Sourcing
- Saga orchestration and compensations
- Per-tenant isolation and outbox pattern
- Idempotent event handlers and replay

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal orchestrator sketch (pseudo)
function startSaga(tenantId, jobId){
  dispatchEvent({tenantId, jobId, type:'IngestRequested'});
}
function onEvent(event){
  if(event.type==='IngestSucceeded') emit('TranscodeRequested', event);
  if(event.type==='IngestFailed' || event.type==='TranscodeFailed' || event.type==='WatermarkFailed'){
    emit('Compensate', {tenantId:event.tenantId, jobId:event.jobId, steps:['Ingest','Transcode','Watermark']});
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ensure exactly-once processing for the tenant outbox?
- How would you test failure injection and crash recovery in this Saga?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-cache invalidation for multi-tenant scales with Redis as the source of truth and Memcached as a secondary layer. Requires concrete event schemas, per-tenant isolation, and practical guards against stale reads.

## Key Concepts

- Cache-invalidation workflow across Redis and Memcached with tenant isolation
- Event schema design (tenantId, articleId, version) and idempotent handling
- Stampede protection (hot-key guards, probabilistic refresh) and recovery strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Redis publish invalidation event
function publishInvalidation(tenantId, articleId, newVersion) {
  const event = { tenantId, articleId, version: newVersion, timestamp: Date.now() };
  redis.publish('cache:invalidate', JSON.stringify(event));
}

// Redis listener and Memcached worker
function handleInvalidation(event) {
  const key = `article:${event.tenantId}:${event.articleId}`;
  redis.del(key);
  redis.incr(`version:${event.tenantId}`);

  // Memcached CAS invalidation
  memcached.gets(key, (err, data, cas) => {
    if (data) memcached.cas(key, '', cas, 0, () => {});
  });
}
```
